              "id": "c1d36c69-0982-30b7-85f9-c5c8f50ae44f",
              "name": "Vulnerability of Predictable Pseudorandom Number Generation",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Vulnerability of Predictable Pseudorandom Number Generation\" in Java refers to the security risk associated with the use of predictable pseudorandom number generators (PRNGs) in security-sensitive contexts. PRNGs are algorithms that use mathematical formulas to produce sequences of random numbers. However, if the seed value used by the PRNG is known or can be guessed, an attacker can predict the numbers that will be generated by the PRNG. This can lead to a variety of security vulnerabilities, such as the ability to guess session identifiers, encryption keys, or other sensitive data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a secure random number generator that is suitable for use in security-sensitive contexts. In Java, this can be achieved by using the `java.security.SecureRandom` class, which provides a cryptographically strong random number generator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `java.util.Random`, use `java.security.SecureRandom` to generate random numbers. Here is an example of how to generate a random number using `java.security.SecureRandom`:\n\n```java\nimport java.security.SecureRandom;\n\nSecureRandom secureRandom = new SecureRandom();\nint randomNumber = secureRandom.nextInt();\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `java.security.SecureRandom` class is part of the Java standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 2017 Category A6 - Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-330: Use of Insufficiently Random Values](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/330.html)"
